{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.319,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "ARITHMETIC PROGRESSIONS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.104,
            0.9,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "53"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.139,
            0.302,
            0.158
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, when"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.161,
            0.765,
            0.293
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} &a = - 7, \\quad d = - 2, \\quad  the AP is   - 7, - 9, - 11, - 13, \\ldots \\\\&a = 1.0, \\quad d = 0.1, \\quad  the AP is   1.0, 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, \\ldots \\\\&a = 0, \\quad d = 1 \\frac{1}{2}, \\quad  the AP is   0, 1 \\frac{1}{2}, 3, 4 \\frac{1}{2}, 6, \\ldots \\\\&a = 2, \\quad d = 0, \\quad  the AP is   2, 2, 2, 2, \\ldots\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.297,
            0.903,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": "So, if you know what a and d are, you can list the AP. What about the other way round? That is, if you are given a list of numbers can you say that it is an AP and then find a and d? Since a is the first term, it can easily be written. We know that in an AP, every succeeding term is obtained by adding d to the preceding term. So, d found by subtracting any term from its succeeding term, i.e., the term which immediately follows it should be same for an AP."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.419,
            0.508,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "For example, for the list of numbers :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.468,
            0.192,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "We have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.444,
            0.592,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}6, 9, 12, 15, \\cdots, \\cdots \\\\a_{2}-a_{1}=9 \\quad 令 6=3, \\\\a_{3}-a_{2}=12-9=3, \\\\a_{4}-a_{3}=15-12=3.\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.541,
            0.902,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "Here the difference of any two consecutive terms in each case is 3. So, the given list is an AP whose first term a is 6 and common difference d is 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.584,
            0.538,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "For the list of numbers : 6, 3, 0, – 3, . . .,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.607,
            0.597,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}a_{2}-a_{1}=3-6=-3 \\\\a_{3}-a_{2}=0-3=-3 \\\\a_{4}-a_{3}=-3-0=-3\\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.682,
            0.901,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": "Similarly this is also an AP whose first term is 6 and the common difference is –3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.728,
            0.583,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "In general, for an AP $a _ { _ 1 } , a _ { _ 2 } , \\ldots , a _ { _ n } ,$ we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.412,
            0.756,
            0.552,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": "d =   a _ {   _ { k   +   1 } } - a _ {   _ { k } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_4_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.781,
            0.755,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "where $a _ { k \\textrm { + } 1 }$ and $a _ { _ k }$ are the ( k + 1)th and the kth terms respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.808,
            0.9,
            0.846
          ],
          "content": "To obtain d in a given AP, we need not find all of $a_{_2}-a_{_1},a_{_3}-a_{_2},a_{_4}-a_{_3},\\cdots.$ It is enough to find only one of them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.852,
            0.902,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "Consider the list of numbers 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, . . . . By looking at it, you can tell that the difference between any two consecutive terms is not the same. So, this is not an AP."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.966,
            0.564,
            0.983
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
